	/** Fast-path recovery for interceptThreatToOwner() above when the
	 * intercepted threat resolves (dies/despawns/etc.) before real combat
	 * ever starts -- see the 2026-07-29 fix comment on
	 * interceptThreatToOwner()'s scheduleTask call in the .cpp. Restores
	 * FOLLOW/STAY/GUARD per standingOrder well before the unrelated
	 * post-combat loot sweep would otherwise get around to it.
	 * @pre { this locked }
	 */
	@local
	public native void recoverFromAbortedIntercept();

	/**
	 * STARTCOMBAT variant: the owner just committed a hostile action rather
	 * than having received damage, so there is no arg1 attacker -- resolve the
	 * owner's current target instead.
	 * @pre { this object is locked }
	 */
	@preLocked
	public native void interceptOwnerHostileAction(CreatureObject owner);

	/**
	 * Companion System bug fix (2026-07-13, real live SIGABRT -- see NOTES.md):
	 * interceptThreatToOwner()/interceptOwnerHostileAction() above are
	 * correctly @preLocked (they mutate this companion's own state --
	 * companionState, addDefender(), executeObjectControllerAction() --
	 * all of which assert this object is locked). But CompanionThreatObserver
	 * fires them from whatever thread is processing the *owner's* own
	 * combat-state change (these observers are registered against the
	 * owner's Observable in CompanionControlDeviceImplementation::spawnObject,
	 * not the companion's) -- that thread only guarantees the owner is
	 * locked, never this companion, so calling the two methods above
	 * directly from the observer always crashed. These two entry points do
	 * NOT require the caller to pre-lock anything; each defers to a
	 * Core::getTaskManager() task that locks this companion first (same
	 * proven pattern as CompanionContainerComponent.cpp's
	 * attemptAutoEquip()), satisfying the real methods' own precondition
	 * correctly instead of crashing on it. CompanionThreatObserver.idl calls
	 * these instead of the two @preLocked methods directly.
	 */
	@dirty
	public native void deferredInterceptThreatToOwner(CreatureObject attacker);

	/** STARTCOMBAT sibling of deferredInterceptThreatToOwner() above -- same
	 * reasoning, defers to interceptOwnerHostileAction() under lock. */
	@dirty
	public native void deferredInterceptOwnerHostileAction(CreatureObject owner);

	/**
	 * Companion System bug fix (2026-07-13, "companion doesn't fire its
	 * equipped weapon" -- see NOTES.md): companions are never spawned
	 * through the normal CreatureTemplate-driven mob-spawn path, so
	 * AiAgentImplementation::setupAttackMaps() (the real engine method that
	 * normally builds primaryAttackMap/defaultAttackMap from
	 * npcTemplate->getPrimaryAttacks()) always bails out immediately --
	 * npcTemplate is always null for a companion -- leaving it with zero
	 * usable attacks no matter what weapon it's holding, so the AI combat
	 * leaf that would fire the weapon always finds nothing to use.
	 * Separately, CreatureObjectImplementation::setWeapon() (what
	 * CompanionContainerComponent's attemptAutoEquip() calls after a
	 * successful equip) only ever updates the base CreatureObject `weapon`
	 * field -- never AiAgent's own `currentWeapon`/`primaryWeapon` fields,
	 * which is what AiAgent::getAttackMap() actually reads to decide which
	 * attack map to use. This method builds a generic, weapon-type-filtered
	 * attack map directly (bypassing npcTemplate entirely, using the same
	 * generic humanoid combat set -- brawlermid + marksmanmid -- real
	 * template NPCs like corsec_trooper use) and marks the given weapon as
	 * both the primary and current weapon so getAttackMap() resolves to it.
	 * Safe to call repeatedly (e.g. every time a new weapon is auto-equipped
	 * or unequipped, with weapon possibly null to fall back to unarmed).
	 * @pre { this object is locked }
	 */
	@preLocked
	public native void refreshCombatAttacks(WeaponObject weapon);

	/**
	 * Companion System (2026-07-13, "manual equip radial" pass) --
	 * explicit, player-triggered equip of an item currently sitting loose
	 * in this companion's inventory, via the new "Equip on Companion"
	 * radial (see TangibleObjectMenuComponent.cpp). Unlike
	 * attemptAutoEquip() (CompanionContainerComponent.cpp -- passive,
	 * fires automatically when a loose item lands in inventory, silent on
	 * failure by design since it's not a deliberate player action), this
	 * sends real error messages back to requester on failure -- the same
	 * feedback a real player's own "Wear" action gives -- since the player
	 * explicitly asked for this. @dirty (no pre-lock requirement --
	 * establishes its own lock internally, same reasoning as
	 * deferredInterceptThreatToOwner() above: the object-menu radial
	 * dispatch thread only guarantees the player/item are relevant, never
	 * this companion).
	 */
	@dirty
	public native void equipItemFromInventory(TangibleObject item, CreatureObject requester);

	/**
	 * Companion System (2026-07-13, "Take Off Companion" radial pass;
	 * redesigned 2026-07-14 to a "Pick Up" semantics per user request --
	 * see NOTES.md for both). The missing counterpart to
	 * equipItemFromInventory() above. Explicit, player-triggered un-equip
	 * of an item currently equipped (containmentType >= 4) on this
	 * companion, via the "Pick Up" radial (see
	 * TangibleObjectMenuComponent.cpp, still radial ID 83). Method name
	 * kept as-is (not renamed to match the new "Pick Up" label) to avoid
	 * an unnecessary .idl churn -- the behavior changed, the identifier
	 * didn't.
	 *
	 * 2026-07-14 update: relocates the item into the REQUESTER's own top-
	 * level inventory container instead of the companion's own nested
	 * "inventory" bag -- mirrors the resolution/locking/error-handling
	 * shape a real player's own "un-equip my gear" action already uses
	 * (TransferItemMiscCommand.h), rather than inventing a new pattern.
	 * This was a user-requested UX simplification: taking gear off a
	 * companion now always lands directly in the same inventory window
	 * the player already has open, with no second (companion-side)
	 * container to discover. The companion's own "inventory" bag object
	 * still exists and is still used for the *other* direction (a
	 * non-equippable item given TO the companion that doesn't auto-equip
	 * still lands there) -- this change only affects the take-off
	 * direction. Sends real success/failure messages back to requester,
	 * same as equipItemFromInventory(). @dirty for the same reason
	 * equipItemFromInventory() is -- establishes its own lock internally,
	 * the object-menu radial dispatch thread only guarantees the
	 * player/item are relevant, never this companion (and, as of this
	 * update, never the requester's own inventory either -- also locked
	 * internally now).
	 */
	@dirty
	public native void unequipItemToInventory(TangibleObject item, CreatureObject requester);

